%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4B98467B] ## Definition
%[text] **Definition**. Consider a square $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}. A vector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"} is said to be an *eigenvector* of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} with a corresponding *eigenvalue* $\\lambda${"altText":"lambda"} if
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}${"altText":"A v = lambda v"}.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Eigenvector in 2-D](text:image:619e)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Most vectors* $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} *will point in a different direction when multiplied by* $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} *(left panel). Eigenvectors are the exception. An eigenvector is any vector that points in the same direction after being multiplied by* $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} *(right panel).*
%[text] ![](text:image:642b) **Try**
%[text] Consider the 2x2 matrix $\\mathbf A${"altText":"A"}:
A = sym([2 -1; -1 2])
%[text] - A vector $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} is defined through its angle with the $x${"altText":"x"}-axis below. Adjust the angle $\\theta${"altText":"theta"} using the slider so that $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} is an eigenvector of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}.
%[text] - Is there more than one eigenvector of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}?
%[text] - Based on the values of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and $\\mathbf{Ax}${"altText":"Ax"}, what are eigenvalues that correspond to the eigenvectors that you found? \
thetad =sym(330);  % In degrees %[control:slider:3304]{"position":[13,16]}
x = sym([cosd(thetad); sind(thetad)]);
Ax = sym(A)*x;
% Plot the vectors
figure
hold on
Setup2DVectorPlot([-2.5 2.5 -2.5 2.5])
PlotVector2D([0 0],double(x),"\mathbf{x}",-0.5)
PlotVector2D([0 0],double(Ax),"\mathbf{Ax}",0.5)
hold off
x
Ax
%[text] ![](text:image:2c4c) **Reflect**
%[text] - Suppose that there is an eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"} that satisfies the definition. Will $-\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"-v"} also satisfy the definition? \
