%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F1B33DBF] ## Solving for eigenvalues and eigenvectors
%[text] You can solve for the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a matrix analytically using two steps:
%[text] 1. Find the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} by solving $\\det(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda \\mathbf{I}\n) = 0${"altText":"det(A-lambda I ) = 0"} for $\\lambda${"altText":"lambda"}.
%[text] 2. Solve for the eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} by solving the linear system $(\\mathbf{A} -  \\lambda \\mathbf{I} )\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}}${"altText":"(A - lambda I )v = 0"} for each $\\lambda${"altText":"lambda"}. \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5EC320F9] ### Eigenvalues 
%[text] To understand the first step (solving for the eigenvalues), consider the definition:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}${"altText":"A v = lambda v"}
%[text] You can rewrite the definition as:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\begin{array}{rl}\n\\mathbf{A v} - \\lambda \\mathbf{v} &= \\mathbf{0} \n\\\\ \n(\\mathbf{A } - \\lambda \\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v} &= \\mathbf{0} \n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: A v - lambda v  = 0 ; (A - lambda I)v  = 0"}
%[text] - In this form, an eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"} solves the matrix equation $(\\mathbf{A } - \\lambda \\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}${"altText":"(A - lambda I)v = 0"}.
%[text] - If the matrix $\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda \\mathbf{I}${"altText":"A-lambda I"} is invertible, then the only solution to this equation is the trivial solution: $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}${"altText":"v = 0"}.
%[text] - Therefore, to find a nontrivial eigenvector, $\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda \\mathbf{I}\n${"altText":"A-lambda I"} must **not** be invertible. \
%[text] Recall that a square matrix is invertible if (and only if) its determinant is nonzero. This implies that (nontrivial) eigenvalues satisfy
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\det(\\mathbf{A}-\\lambda \\mathbf{I}\n) = 0${"altText":"det(A-lambda I ) = 0"}
%[text] The left-hand side (LHS) of this equation yields a polynomial function of $\\lambda${"altText":"lambda"}, called the *characteristic polynomial*. Solving the characteristic polynomial equation gives the eigenvalues.
%[text] ![](text:image:1560) **Example**. Solve for the eigenvalues of 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \]"}.
%[text] **Solution.** The eigenvalues can be obtained by solving $\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0${"altText":"det(A - lambda I) = 0"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I} = \n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc} \n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)\n-\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc} \n\\lambda & 0\\\\\n0 & \\lambda\n\\end{array}\\right)\n=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc} \n2 - \\lambda & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2 - \\lambda\n\\end{array}\\right)\n${"altText":"A - lambda I = matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \] - matrix \[ lambda , 0; 0 , lambda \] = matrix \[ 2 - lambda , -1; -1 , 2 - lambda \]"}, 
%[text]     so: $\\det( \\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I} )= \\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 - \\lambda & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2 - \\lambda\n\\end{array}\\right|\n= (2-\\lambda)(2-\\lambda) - (-1)(-1) = \\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda +3${"altText":"det( A - lambda I )= |matrix \[ 2 - lambda , -1; -1 , 2 - lambda \]| = (2-lambda)(2-lambda) - (-1)(-1) = lambda^2 - 4lambda +3"}
%[text] Setting the characteristic polynomial equal to zero and solving yields:
%[text]  $\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 3 = 0 \\\\ \\lambda = 1,  3${"altText":"lambda^2 - 4lambda + 3 = 0 ; lambda = 1, 3"}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:82ad) **Exercise**. Solve for the eigenvalues of
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \]"}
%[text] in MATLAB.
%[text] ![](text:image:56bc) **Pro-tip**. You can solve for eigenvalues of a numeric matrix `A` in MATLAB using the [`eig`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/eig.html) function. The basic syntax is
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] [V,D] = eig(A)
%[text] ```
%[text] The result of this function is two matrices: `V`, whose columns are the eigenvectors of `A`, and `D`, a diagonal matrix that contains the eigenvalues.

%%
%[text] ![](text:image:88a9) **Exercise**. Consider the matrix
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{B} = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 &  2\\\\\n-3 & -6\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"B = matrix \[ 1 , 2; -3 , -6 \]"}.
%[text] (a)  Solve for the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{B}${"altText":"B"} by hand.
%[text] (b) Check your result by solving for the eigenvalues in MATLAB.


%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D2AFA43B] ### **Eigenvectors**
%[text] After identifying the eigenvalues, you can solve for the eigenvectors by solving the linear system 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $(\\mathbf{A} -  \\lambda \\mathbf{I} )\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}}${"altText":"(A - lambda I )v = 0"}
%[text] for each $\\lambda${"altText":"lambda"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:98ba) **Example**. Solve for the eigenvector of
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \]"}
%[text] that corresponds to $\\lambda = 1${"altText":"lambda = 1"}. 
%[text] **Solution**.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $(\\mathbf{A} -  1 \\mathbf{I})\\mathbf{v} = 0 \n\\quad\n\\rightarrow \n\\quad\n\\left\[\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)\n-\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 &  0\\\\\n0 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\right\]\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\nv\_1 \\\\\nv\_2 \n\\end{array}\\right)\n=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n0 \\\\\n0\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\n\\quad\n\\rightarrow \n\\quad\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\nv\_1 \\\\\nv\_2 \n\\end{array}\\right)\n=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n0 \\\\\n0\n\\end{array}\\right)\n${"altText":"(A - 1 I)v = 0 -\> \[ matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \] - matrix \[ 1 , 0; 0 , 1 \] \] matrix \[ v\_1 ; v\_2 \] = matrix \[ 0 ; 0 \] -\> matrix \[ 1 , -1; -1 , 1 \] matrix \[ v\_1 ; v\_2 \] = matrix \[ 0 ; 0 \]"}
%[text] Write the augmented matrix and row-reduce:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right.\n\\ \\left| \\ \\begin{array}{cc}\n0 \\\\\n0\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\quad \\rightarrow \\quad\n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 & -1\\\\\n0 & 0\n\\end{array}\\right.\n\\ \\left| \\ \\begin{array}{cc}\n0 \\\\\n0\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"(matrix \[ 1 , -1; -1 , 1 \] | matrix \[ 0 ; 0 \]) -\> (matrix \[ 1 , -1; 0 , 0 \] | matrix \[ 0 ; 0 \])"}
%[text] This implies that the solutions satisfy:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $v\_1 - v\_2 = 0${"altText":"v\_1 - v\_2 = 0"}
%[text] The choice of $v\_1${"altText":"v\_1"} is arbitrary. Setting $v\_1 = 1${"altText":"v\_1 = 1"} implies $v\_2 = v\_1 = 1${"altText":"v\_2 = v\_1 = 1"}, yielding the eigenvector:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{v} = \\left( \\matrix{ 1 \\cr 1 }\\right)${"altText":"v = ( matrix \[ 1 ; 1 \])"}.
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:6fc7) **Exercise**. Solve for the eigenvector of 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \]"}
%[text] that corresponds to $\\lambda = 3${"altText":"lambda = 3"}. 
showSoln = false; %[control:checkbox:2806]{"position":[12,17]}
A = sym([2 -1; -1 2]);  
if(showSoln)
    [V,D] = eig(A);
    v = V(:,2)
end
%%
