%[text] %[text:anchor:H_039C6562] ## Eigen Decomposition
%[text] **Definition**. A $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} with $n${"altText":"n"} linearly independent eigenvectors can be decomposed as
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{V \\Lambda V}^{-1}${"altText":"A = V Lambda V^(-1)"},
%[text] where $\\mathbf{V}${"altText":"V"} is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and $\\mathbf{\\Lambda}${"altText":"Lambda"} is a diagonal matrix that contains the corresponding eigenvalues:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{V} =\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_1\n\\\\\n\\\\\n\\\\\n\\end{array} \\right| \n\\left. \\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_2\n\\\\\n\\\\\n\\\\\n\\end{array}\\right|\n\\left. \\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\n\\cdots\n\\\\ \\\\ \\\\\n\\end{array}\\right|\n\\left. \\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_n\n\\\\\n\\\\\n\\\\\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"V = (column v\_1 | column v\_2| column ...| column v\_n)"}   and   $\\mathbf\\Lambda =\n\\left( \\matrix{\n\\lambda\_1 & & & \\mathbf{0}\n\\cr\n & \\lambda\_2 & & \n\\cr\n & & \\ddots & \n\\cr\n\\mathbf{0} & & & \\lambda\_n\n}\n\\right)${"altText":"Lambda = ( matrix \[ lambda\_1 , , , 0 ; , lambda\_2 , , ; , , diagonal continuation , ; 0 , , , lambda\_n \] )"}.
%[text] This factorization is often referred to as *matrix diagonalization*.
%[text] ![](text:image:71a0) **Example**. Consider 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n2 & -1\\\\\n-1 & 2\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ 2 , -1; -1 , 2 \]"}.
%[text] (a) Is $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} diagonalizable? (b) If so, solve for the matrix diagonalization.
%[text] **Solution**.
%[text] (a) A set of eigenvectors (paired with the eigenvalues) is 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\lambda\_1 = 1, \\ \\mathbf{v}\_1 = \\left( \\matrix{ 1 \\cr 1} \\right)${"altText":"lambda\_1 = 1, v\_1 = ( matrix \[ 1 ; 1\] )"}     and     $\\lambda\_2 = 3, \\ \\mathbf{v}\_2 = \\left( \\matrix{ 1 \\cr -1} \\right)${"altText":"lambda\_2 = 3, v\_2 = ( matrix \[ 1 ; -1\] )"}
%[text] Because $\\mathbf{v}\_1${"altText":"v\_1"} and $\\mathbf{v}\_2${"altText":"v\_2"} are linearly independent, $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} is diagonalizable.
%[text] (b) Using the eigenvalues/eigenvector pairs:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{V \\Lambda V}^{-1}${"altText":"A = V Lambda V^(-1)"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_1\n\\\\ \\\\ \\\\\n\\end{array} \\right| \n\\left. \\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_2\n\\\\ \\\\ \\\\\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n\\lambda\_1 & 0 \n\\\\\n0 & \\lambda\_2\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_1\n\\\\ \\\\ \\\\\n\\end{array} \\right| \n\\left. \\begin{array}{c}\n\\\\\nv\_2\n\\\\ \\\\ \\\\\n\\end{array}\\right)^{-1}\n${"altText":"= (column v\_1 | column v\_2) matrix \[ lambda\_1 , 0 ; 0 , lambda\_2 \] (column v\_1 | column v\_2)^(-1)"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n1 &1 \\\\\n1 & -1\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n1 & 0 \n\\\\\n0 & 3\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n1 &1 \\\\\n1 & -1\n\\end{array}\\right)^{-1}\n${"altText":"= matrix \[ 1 , 1 ; 1 , -1 \] matrix \[ 1 , 0 ; 0 , 3 \] matrix \[ 1 , 1 ; 1 , -1 \]^(-1)"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n1 &1 \\\\\n1 & -1\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n1 & 0 \n\\\\\n0 & 3\n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{c}\n1/2 & 1/2 \\\\\n1/2 & -1/2\n\\end{array}\\right)\n${"altText":"= matrix \[ 1 , 1 ; 1 , -1 \] matrix \[ 1 , 0 ; 0 , 3 \] matrix \[ 1\/2 , 1\/2 ; 1\/2 , -1\/2 \]"}
%[text] ![](text:image:877c) **Reflect**
