%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C30064E4] ## Eigenvalues of non-invertible matrices
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_837C6034] Suppose that a matrix has an eigenvalue $\\lambda = 0${"altText":"lambda = 0"}. For that eigenvalue:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\det(\\mathbf{A}-0\\mathbf{I}\n) = \\det(\\mathbf{A}) = 0${"altText":"det(A-0I ) = det(A) = 0"}.
%[text] This implies that the matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} is not invertible if any eigenvalue is zero. So, for $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} to be invertible, all the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} must be nonzero.
%[text] **Theorem**. A $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix is invertible if and only if it has $n${"altText":"n"} nonzero eigenvalues.
