%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6B3CBC0A] ## Defective matrices
%[text] **Definition**. An $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix that does not have $n${"altText":"n"} linearly independent eigenvectors is called *defective*. 
%[text] - A defective matrix cannot be diagonalized.
%[text] - Some invertible matrices (like the *Jordan matrix*, shown below) are defective. \
%[text] ![](text:image:27cb) **Example**. Consider the $3 \\times 3${"altText":"3 times 3"} Jordan matrix $\\mathbf{J}${"altText":"J"} defined below.
J = sym(diag([4 4 4]) + diag([1 1],1))
  %[control:button:2e3f]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] Compute the eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{J}${"altText":"J"} using [`eig`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/eig.html)`.`
[V,D] = eig(J)
%[text] Notice that the eigenvector output of `eig` has only one eigenvector. This occurred because the matrix is defective and has only one independent eigenvector. However, this matrix is invertible. Consider the determinant of $\\mathbf{J}${"altText":"J"} computed below.
detJ = det(J)
%[text] Notice that the determinant of $\\mathbf{J}${"altText":"J"} is equal to the product of the eigenvalues. This is generally true:
%[text] - The determinant of a matrix equals the product of its eigenvalues.  \
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4039) **Exercise**. Consider the matrices defined below.
D = sym(diag([2 2 3]))
K = sym(diag([2 2 3]) + diag([1 1],1))
  %[control:button:3ae5]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] For each matrix determine:
%[text] 1. Is the matrix defective?
%[text] 2. Is the matrix invertible? \
